[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one bug: the parser reads dates month first. The reports
merged into it describe three. Each now has its own ticket.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is marked known with one fix queued. That fix covers <code>parser</code> only.
The other two keep coming after it ships.
</Callout>

## The three problems

**`parser`: day and month flipped.** The shared parser reads every date month first. Every
tenant on file writes the day first, so 7 April comes out as 4 July. It covers every tenant and every
channel except the booking-v3 form.

**`hint`: the booking-v3 form tells people to type month first.** booking-v3 prints
`MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, then reads the date the tenant's way, day first. People
type what the hint says and the date flips. Only tenants on booking-v3, only through the
form. The parser fix does not touch it: the fix is the hint.

**`shift`: the date moves by one day.** These reports are not a flip. Tuesday 6 October shows
as Monday; a client in Lisbon gets Tuesday and one in Toronto gets Monday; an invite arrives
before the booking was made. That reads as a time-zone or offset bug in how the invite date is
built. The desk spotted these (desk notes) but filed them under `parser` by tenant.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line. All three
problems produce the same subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong"), so subject
matching cannot tell them apart. Intake's tag cannot either: it guesses from the reporter's
browser, and the table counts how often it disagrees.

Sort a date report by its **tenant** (locale and booking form), its **channel**, and **what
the body says happened** (flip or one-day move), not by its subject. 0001 stays as it is; a
new decision record should replace it.

